answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a<0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381948} :answer-field/id #uuid "2562ec6c-3e79-5071-970e-b694b6b736ac" :answer-field/key "field-6" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381947}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2d8829ca-0ab5-57ff-a0ec-1417036e9deb" :question/math-academy-id "q-334285" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074855/assets/48d8520fefe8c0d38decf417ababd94771945b802efce565a4384d4d22026409.png)\n\nThe phase portrait for a system of linear differential equations whose matrix has two complex eigenvalues $\\lambda =a\\pm b\\text{i}$ is shown above. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.The given curve is \n{{field-1}},\nrepresenting\n{{field-2}}\nthat is traversed \n{{field-3}}.\nThus, we must have \n{{field-4}}.\n\nThe spiral is traversed toward \n{{field-5}}\nas $t\\to \\infty .$\nHence, \n{{field-6}}." :question/worked-solution "Let $v=p+q\\text{i},$ where $p,q\\in {R}^{2},$ be an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =a+b\\text{i}.$\n\nThen, a complex solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t)=v{e}^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nUsing Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t) & =v{e}^{(a+b\\text{i})t} \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt))(p+q\\text{i}) \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)+\\text{i}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\text{x}(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)+{c}_{2}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q),\n$$\n\nwhere the parts $(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)$ and $(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q)$ are bounded.\n\nNow notice that the given curve is $\\text{unbounded},$ representing $\\text{a spiral}$ that is traversed $\\text{counterclockwise}.$ Thus, we must have $a\\ne 0.$\n\nAlso, notice that the spiral is traversed toward $\\text{the origin}.$\n\nHence, to insure that ${e}^{at}\\to 0,$ we require $a<0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381950 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f0b176c-c802-5b3b-85d0-dede602c9bf3" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Systems of Linear Differential Equations With Complex Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aa53582d-3f0e-54cb-96c2-32ecbe1b010b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{Im}(\\lambda )<0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381952} {:answer/id #uuid "c910e0ea-997a-5729-a1ca-42b81086b64a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{Re}(\\lambda )=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381953} {:answer/id #uuid "82f7a350-5955-5d9d-a743-ee28fdc7217e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{Im}(\\lambda )=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381954} {:answer/id #uuid "cf356728-252d-5e9b-9ca1-7f2326f11a1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{Im}(\\lambda )>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381955} {:answer/id #uuid "dfc7128a-f588-5d14-9f01-5d4c77eae705" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{Re}(\\lambda )<0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381956} {:answer/id #uuid "10099658-46d0-5b2a-9525-b90a1da9a419" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{Re}(\\lambda )>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381957}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c910e0ea-997a-5729-a1ca-42b81086b64a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{Re}(\\lambda )=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381953} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e81c1b1-1a6e-5ccd-b435-d56edcac035d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381951} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b49b637-cf14-59df-896b-6f3f110d0116" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a stable spiral (sink)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381959} {:answer/id #uuid "47031009-738f-578b-927b-d7b003564c80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a center" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381960} {:answer/id #uuid "2fe75dee-c1ce-5148-aa23-dc54cf323083" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an unstable spiral (source)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381961}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "47031009-738f-578b-927b-d7b003564c80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a center" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381960} :answer-field/id #uuid "66a4630a-bacf-59d1-9abe-6d8e6bde89a1" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381958}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a31704fc-1a42-57e7-80da-b24092ca3cb2" :question/math-academy-id "q-334315" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-5y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=5x(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda =\\pm 5\\text{i}.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.$\\,$\nSince \n{{field-1}},\nthe equilibrium point (origin) is {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Let $v=p+q\\text{i},$ where $p,q\\in {R}^{2},$ be an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =a+b\\text{i}.$\n\nThen, a complex solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t)=v{e}^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nUsing Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t) & =v{e}^{(a+b\\text{i})t} \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt))(p+q\\text{i}) \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)+\\text{i}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\text{x}(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)+{c}_{2}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q),\n$$\n\nwhere the parts $(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)$ and $(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q)$ are bounded.\n\nNow, notice that $a=\\text{Re}(\\lambda )=0$ and ${e}^{at}={e}^{0\\cdot t}=1$ for all $t.$ Thus, we have an ellipse that curves around the origin. \n\nTherefore, since $\\text{Re}(\\lambda )=0,$ the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{a center}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381962 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f0b176c-c802-5b3b-85d0-dede602c9bf3" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Systems of Linear Differential Equations With Complex Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b07e97a4-b43a-5ee4-b91c-5df234741088" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{Im}(\\lambda )<0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381964} {:answer/id #uuid "af1adfed-8856-5971-9624-5dbcdbcad873" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{Re}(\\lambda )=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381965} {:answer/id #uuid "85d9a61d-c0a8-5fa7-85e9-a5aba5f072e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{Im}(\\lambda )=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381966} {:answer/id #uuid "911eb3fb-cd1f-5798-8131-4f8c15f3bf43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{Im}(\\lambda )>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381967} {:answer/id #uuid "7f0e614a-9da1-52a3-a3f8-e9cd1ecb892d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{Re}(\\lambda )<0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381968} {:answer/id #uuid "6af6df31-845d-5d9c-8df5-45f3be8d8174" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{Re}(\\lambda )>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381969}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "af1adfed-8856-5971-9624-5dbcdbcad873" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{Re}(\\lambda )=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381965} :answer-field/id #uuid "04e05e1b-b2f6-586c-8f6a-289ba15261eb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381963} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c307aadd-2449-591c-a87b-c5c7e09c2a2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a stable spiral (sink)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381971} {:answer/id #uuid "b9655560-2a5a-5ef2-9b80-5e7cfbedadc1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a center" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381972} {:answer/id #uuid "68706ede-51b7-5043-a7d0-5a98b90ba94f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an unstable spiral (source)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381973}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b9655560-2a5a-5ef2-9b80-5e7cfbedadc1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a center" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381972} :answer-field/id #uuid "277ac021-1e07-5af2-adde-e36f7c76ee18" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381970}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6fc494c0-c0db-5717-95ae-93caa2f16214" :question/math-academy-id "q-334318" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-3y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=12x(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda =\\pm 6\\text{i}.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.$\\,$\nSince \n{{field-1}},\nthe equilibrium point (origin) is {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Let $v=p+q\\text{i},$ where $p,q\\in {R}^{2},$ be an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =a+b\\text{i}.$\n\nThen, a complex solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t)=v{e}^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nUsing Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t) & =v{e}^{(a+b\\text{i})t} \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt))(p+q\\text{i}) \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)+\\text{i}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\text{x}(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)+{c}_{2}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q),\n$$\n\nwhere the parts $(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)$ and $(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q)$ are bounded.\n\nNow, notice that $a=\\text{Re}(\\lambda )=0$ and ${e}^{at}={e}^{0\\cdot t}=1$ for all $t.$ Thus, we have an ellipse that curves around the origin. \n\nTherefore, since $\\text{Re}(\\lambda )=0,$ the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{a center}.$"}]}
